{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0125FmqcCzEeT5vvnXer5gex","type":"tool_result","content":"60:DIF analysis has a long history in educational testing \\cite{holland1993differential,zumbo1999handbook}, where it identifies items that function differently across demographic groups. The Mantel--Haenszel procedure (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959; Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) and the ETS classification system \\cite{zieky1993practical} are standard tools. Item-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item} extend this to continuous covariates.\n63:Growing Pains \\cite{polo2024growing} uses IRT-based anchor items for efficient evaluation. Our work is complementary: DIF analysis identifies which anchor items may be degrading. Benchmark contamination studies typically treat items as having fixed properties; our work questions this assumption directly.\n84:We compute the $L_z$ person-fit statistic \\cite{drasgow1985appropriateness} under a unidimensional 2PL IRT model. Values $|L_z| > 2$ indicate model-misfit. We also fit a bifactor model with one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors, reporting RMSEA, CFI, TLI, and SRMR.\n212:We replicate temporal DIF on GSM8K and correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap \\cite{zhang2024gsm1k} as an external ground truth (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k}).\n237:Item-Focused Trees (IFT) \\cite{tutz2016item} use release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the arbitrary categorical split of MH-DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:ift})."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"04046084-1760-4ca2-9f60-9541c89668b3","uuid":"60494587-0b63-4e9e-abc2-2df5ebc0b75d","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:56:56.429Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"60:DIF analysis has a long history in educational testing \\cite{holland1993differential,zumbo1999handbook}, where it identifies items that function differently across demographic groups. The Mantel--Haenszel procedure (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959; Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) and the ETS classification system \\cite{zieky1993practical} are standard tools. Item-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item} extend this to continuous covariates.\n63:Growing Pains \\cite{polo2024growing} uses IRT-based anchor items for efficient evaluation. Our work is complementary: DIF analysis identifies which anchor items may be degrading. Benchmark contamination studies typically treat items as having fixed properties; our work questions this assumption directly.\n84:We compute the $L_z$ person-fit statistic \\cite{drasgow1985appropriateness} under a unidimensional 2PL IRT model. Values $|L_z| > 2$ indicate model-misfit. We also fit a bifactor model with one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors, reporting RMSEA, CFI, TLI, and SRMR.\n212:We replicate temporal DIF on GSM8K and correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap \\cite{zhang2024gsm1k} as an external ground truth (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k}).\n237:Item-Focused Trees (IFT) \\cite{tutz2016item} use release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the arbitrary categorical split of MH-DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}).","numLines":5}}
