{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NqFzAe4534o95wZfGLnPQv","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/worst/paper.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"04046084-1760-4ca2-9f60-9541c89668b3","uuid":"ac7641c8-8fe8-4364-bd07-e009ecb771e1","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:57:18.671Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/worst/paper.tex","oldString":"\\cite{zhang2024gsm1k}","newString":"(Zhang et al., 2024)","originalFile":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\title{Psychometric Audit of LLM Benchmark Stability:\\\\Measurement Invariance Violations in MMLU Across Model Generations}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLarge Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume measurement invariance --- that item difficulty is stable across model populations. We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), comparing temporal cohorts (pre-2024 vs.\\ 2024-H1). We find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category, $|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$), far exceeding the 0.18\\% random-split baseline ($z = 100.3$, $p < 0.001$). However, this instability does not threaten ranking validity: the Spearman correlation between full and stable-only rankings is $\\rho = 0.997$, revealing massive measurement redundancy. A psychometric model audit shows 98\\% of models exhibit person-misfit ($|L_z| > 2$) under unidimensional Item Response Theory (IRT), and a bifactor model (general $+$ 57 subject factors) fails all fit indices (CFI $= 0.72$, RMSEA $= 0.089$). Cross-benchmark replication on six benchmarks shows DIF rates vary from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%, reflecting benchmark-specific measurement properties rather than a universal phenomenon. We provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) and compare DIF against ConStat and DVD detectors, finding low overlap (Jaccard $= 0.18$ and $0.09$ respectively) --- these methods detect different constructs. We conclude that MMLU systematically violates measurement invariance assumptions, and that existing unidimensional IRT frameworks are fundamentally misspecified for multi-domain LLM benchmarks.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nWhen researchers report that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU by 5 points,'' they implicitly assume that both scores reflect the same underlying construct measured under equivalent conditions. In psychometrics, this assumption is formalized as \\emph{measurement invariance}: item difficulty should not depend on which population of examinees is tested, after controlling for ability (Holland \\& Thayer, 1993). Violations of measurement invariance --- detected through Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis --- indicate that items function differently across groups, undermining score comparability.\n\nThe LLM evaluation ecosystem has grown rapidly, with benchmarks like MMLU (Hendrycks et al., 2021) serving as de facto standards for comparing model capabilities. Yet the psychometric properties of these benchmarks have received little scrutiny. Models released in 2024 differ from 2023 models in training data, scale, and alignment procedures. If benchmark items function differently across these generations, temporal score comparisons may be misleading.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF analysis (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959; Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) to the METABENCH response matrix (Perlitz et al., 2024) --- 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items --- the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our primary finding is not about contamination detection, but about the systematic violation of measurement assumptions that LLM benchmarks implicitly rely upon.\n\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF, but this instability has negligible impact on rankings ($\\rho = 0.997$), revealing massive redundancy. More fundamentally, 98\\% of models show person-misfit under unidimensional IRT ($|L_z| > 2$), and a bifactor model with 57 subject-specific factors fails all standard fit criteria (CFI $= 0.72$, TLI $= 0.68$, RMSEA $= 0.089$). These results indicate that unidimensional IRT --- the implicit model underlying MH-DIF and many evaluation assumptions --- is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject multidimensional structure.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Psychometric audit at scale}: 31.3\\% temporal DIF on 12,508 items with 98\\% person-misfit, demonstrating systematic measurement invariance violations.\n    \\item \\textbf{Measurement model characterization}: Bifactor IRT failure and cross-benchmark DIF variation (17.9--31.3\\%) reveal benchmark-specific measurement properties.\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: Cross-subject matching with $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$, portable across benchmarks.\n    \\item \\textbf{Diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) but rankings are robust ($\\rho = 0.997$), characterizing MMLU's redundancy structure.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.}\nData contamination --- where training data overlaps with evaluation items --- has been documented extensively (Dodge et al., 2021; Li et al., 2024; Deng et al., 2024). Detection methods include $n$-gram overlap (Brown et al., 2020), Min-$K$\\% (Shi et al., 2024), and perturbation-based approaches like ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024). These methods validate against web-overlap labels, which measure global exposure rather than differential item functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometrics for LLMs.}\nRecent work has applied IRT to LLM evaluation (Polo et al., 2024; Vania et al., 2021), using item parameters for efficient evaluation (Zhuang et al., 2024). However, the core assumption of these approaches --- that items have stable parameters across model populations --- has not been empirically tested at scale.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in Educational Testing.}\nDIF analysis has a long history in educational testing (Holland \\& Thayer, 1993; Zumbo, 1999), where it identifies items that function differently across demographic groups. The Mantel--Haenszel procedure (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959; Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) and the ETS classification system (Zieky, 1993) are standard tools. Item-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) extend this to continuous covariates.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Validity.}\nGrowing Pains (Polo et al., 2024b) uses IRT-based anchor items for efficient evaluation. Our work is complementary: DIF analysis identifies which anchor items may be degrading. Benchmark contamination studies typically treat items as having fixed properties; our work questions this assumption directly.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF}\n\nFor each item $i$, we partition models into reference (pre-2024, $N_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}253$) and focal (2024-H1, $N_{\\mathrm{foc}} = 656$) cohorts. Models are stratified by total score quintiles. The MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\alpha_{\\mathrm{MH}} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2 \\times 2$ table at ability stratum $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The DIF measure is $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MH}})$. Items are classified using the ETS system: A ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}| < 1.5$), and C ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\nWe inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses for a fraction $p$ of ``contaminated'' items in the focal cohort. The key innovation is \\emph{cross-subject matching}: using total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the MH conditioning variable, eliminating matching variable contamination ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$).\n\nDetection power is measured as $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$, isolating the detection gain from injection. We also report $\\Delta$FPR to verify that injection does not inflate false positives.\n\n\\subsection{Person-Fit and Model-Fit Diagnostics}\n\nWe compute the $L_z$ person-fit statistic (Drasgow et al., 1985) under a unidimensional 2PL IRT model. Values $|L_z| > 2$ indicate model-misfit. We also fit a bifactor model with one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors, reporting RMSEA, CFI, TLI, and SRMR.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} presents the main temporal DIF results on the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 items after filtering).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & $N_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ & $N_{\\mathrm{foc}}$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Rand.\\%} & \\textbf{Enr.} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal 23v24 & 1,253 & 656 & 31.3 & 0.18 & 0.96 \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2,610 & 2,610 & 2.9 & 0.18 & 1.19 \\\\\nWithin-fam.\\ Llama & 161 & 308 & 61.3 & --- & --- \\\\\nWithin-fam.\\ Mistral & 106 & 84 & 38.7 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary. C\\% = percentage of items in ETS C category ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). Random control: 0.18\\%. Enrichment vs.\\ Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe temporal cohort comparison yields 31.3\\% ETS C items, far exceeding the random-split control (0.18\\%). The ability-quintile comparison produces only 2.9\\% C items, confirming that DIF is driven by temporal membership rather than ability differences. Within-family comparisons show higher rates (61.3\\% for Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3, 38.7\\% for Mistral vs.\\ Mixtral), consistent with larger ability gaps within families. Enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.96 --- near unity --- indicating that DIF flags are orthogonal to web-overlap contamination labels. Person-fit analysis reveals 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, indicating severe misfit under unidimensional IRT.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:domain} shows domain-level variation. DIF rates range from 20.7\\% (computer security) to 48.4\\% (business ethics), with a modest correlation between domain accuracy change and DIF rate ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Subject} & \\textbf{C\\%} & $n$ & $\\Delta$\\textbf{acc} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBusiness ethics & 48.4 & 100 & 0.12 \\\\\nHuman aging & 41.9 & 223 & 0.09 \\\\\nFormal logic & 40.4 & 126 & 0.07 \\\\\nComputer security & 20.7 & 100 & 0.11 \\\\\nMarketing & 20.9 & 234 & 0.06 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Domain-level DIF variation (top/bottom 5 subjects by C\\%). $\\Delta$acc = domain accuracy change between cohorts.}\n\\label{tab:domain}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null Distribution}\n\\label{sec:permutation}\n\nWe establish a null distribution for C\\% via 1,000 random permutations of temporal labels, preserving group sizes (Table~\\ref{tab:permutation}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Statistic} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n\\midrule\nNull mean C\\% & 1.2 \\\\\nNull SD & 0.3 \\\\\nNull max & 2.1 \\\\\nObserved C\\% & 31.3 \\\\\n$z$-score & 100.3 \\\\\n$p$-value & $< 0.001$ \\\\\nRandom split control & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Permutation null distribution for C\\% (1,000 iterations). The observed 31.3\\% is over 100 standard deviations from the null mean.}\n\\label{tab:permutation}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe observed C\\% of 31.3\\% is 100.3 standard deviations above the null mean of 1.2\\% ($p < 0.001$). We note that the permutation null mean (1.2\\%) is higher than the random-split control (0.18\\%), likely reflecting group-size imbalance effects (1,253 vs.\\ 656). Statistical significance is unambiguous, though the simple permutation does not preserve temporal autocorrelation structure.\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{sec:architecture}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models). Table~\\ref{tab:architecture} shows that restricting to decoder-only models produces identical DIF results (Jaccard $= 1.0$), but IRT fit remains poor.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Pop.} & $N$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{RMSEA} & \\textbf{CFI} & $|L_z|{>}2$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull & 5,227 & 31.3 & 0.089 & 0.72 & 98.0\\% \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5,226 & 31.3 & 0.087 & 0.73 & 97.8\\% \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & --- & 0.0 & $-$0.002 & +0.01 & $-$0.2 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Architecture stratification. Restricting to decoder-only models yields identical C\\% but negligible IRT fit improvement.}\n\\label{tab:architecture}\n\\end{table}\n\nThis result resolves the architecture heterogeneity concern but simultaneously confirms that IRT misfit is intrinsic to MMLU's structure, not an artifact of mixing architectures. The 98\\% person-misfit rate persists even in an architecturally homogeneous population.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Temporal DIF}\n\\label{sec:cross_benchmark}\n\nWe replicate temporal DIF analysis on six benchmarks in METABENCH (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_bench}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Bench.} & $n_{\\mathrm{items}}$ & $N_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ & $N_{\\mathrm{foc}}$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Rand.\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 0.18 \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 & 0.32 \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 & 0.37 \\\\\nARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 & 0.60 \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 0.30 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 & 0.28 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark temporal DIF. All random controls are below 1\\% (minimum enrichment ratio: 45$\\times$). C\\% varies nearly 2$\\times$ across benchmarks.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\end{table}\n\nAll six benchmarks show DIF rates far exceeding their random controls (minimum enrichment: 45$\\times$), confirming temporal DIF is a real phenomenon across benchmarks. However, C\\% varies substantially (17.9--31.3\\%, coefficient of variation $= 0.22$), suggesting DIF rates are benchmark-specific measurement properties rather than a universal constant. MMLU's higher rate may reflect its multidimensional 57-subject structure; single-domain benchmarks (GSM8K: 18.9\\%, HellaSwag: 17.9\\%) show lower rates.\n\n\\subsection{GSM8K Replication and GSM1K Correlation}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nWe replicate temporal DIF on GSM8K and correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap \\cite{zhang2024gsm1k} as an external ground truth (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal C\\% & 18.9 & Lower than MMLU \\\\\nSemi-synth.\\ $\\Delta$TPR & 0.818 & Strong (artificial) \\\\\nGSM1K $r$ & $-$0.478 & \\textbf{Negative} \\\\\nGSM1K $N$ & 33 & Small sample \\\\\nGSM1K CI upper $\\rho$ & $-$0.031 & Near zero possible \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{GSM8K replication summary. The GSM1K correlation is negative, opposing the contamination hypothesis.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe GSM8K temporal C\\% of 18.9\\% confirms the DIF phenomenon generalizes beyond MMLU, and the semi-synthetic framework is portable ($\\Delta$TPR $= 0.818$). However, the GSM1K correlation is \\emph{negative} ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$): models with more DIF-flagged items show \\emph{smaller} GSM8K--GSM1K performance gaps. This opposes a naive contamination interpretation. The negative correlation may reflect that DIF, by conditioning on ability, identifies items where high-ability models disproportionately improve --- not contamination indicators per se. However, with $N = 33$ and the upper bound of the Spearman CI at $\\rho = -0.031$, the true effect could be near zero. We treat this as an exploratory finding, not a core claim.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) use release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the arbitrary categorical split of MH-DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{IFT} & \\textbf{MH} & \\textbf{Interp.} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\% flagged & 27.3 & 31.3 & MH detects 4pp more \\\\\nConcordance & --- & --- & 0.74 \\\\\nGradual-only & 412 & 0 & MH misses \\\\\nAbrupt-only & 0 & 289 & MH artifacts? \\\\\nModel fit & 0.094 & 98\\% & Both poor \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH comparison. IFT detects 27.3\\% of items with significant temporal splits, 4pp fewer than MH. Concordance is 0.74 (26\\% disagreement).}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT flags 27.3\\% of items with significant splits (median optimal split: October 2023, SD $= 89$ days), modestly lower than MH's 31.3\\%. The 74\\% concordance indicates substantial agreement but 26\\% disagreement. IFT uniquely identifies 412 items with gradual DIF that MH's categorical split misses, while 289 items are flagged only by MH, potentially reflecting artifacts of the binary temporal split. IFT's own RMSEA ($0.094$) is also poor, reinforcing that no IRT-based method adequately fits MMLU's latent structure.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}\n\\label{sec:semi_synthetic}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semi_synth} presents semi-synthetic injection results with both standard (within-subject) and cross-subject matching.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Match.} & $p$ & \\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{FPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nCross-subj. & 0.0 & 0.326 & 0.314 & --- & --- \\\\\nCross-subj. & 0.3 & 0.548 & 0.314 & 0.222 & 0.000 \\\\\nCross-subj. & 0.5 & 0.834 & 0.314 & 0.508 & 0.000 \\\\\nCross-subj. & 1.0 & 1.000 & 0.314 & 0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\nStandard & 0.0 & 0.298 & 0.298 & --- & --- \\\\\nStandard & 1.0 & 0.934 & 0.616 & 0.636 & 0.318 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic injection results. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$); standard matching inflates FPR by $+0.318$.}\n\\label{tab:semi_synth}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all injection levels while maintaining monotonically increasing $\\Delta$TPR (0.222 at $p = 0.3$ to 0.674 at $p = 1.0$). Standard matching shows FPR inflation of $+0.318$ at full injection, confirming matching variable contamination. The baseline FPR of 0.314 reflects the temporal DIF already present in the real data, not methodological error. The dose--response relationship is monotonic, supporting the framework's internal validity. We acknowledge that the injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination processes.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{sec:bifactor}\n\nWe attempt a bifactor IRT model with one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors to address the unidimensional IRT misspecification (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Index} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.089 & $< 0.06$ & Fail \\\\\nCFI & 0.72 & $> 0.90$ & Fail \\\\\nTLI & 0.68 & $> 0.90$ & Fail \\\\\nSRMR & 0.11 & $< 0.08$ & Fail \\\\\nGeneral factor \\% & 31 & $> 50$ & Fail \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit indices. The model did not converge within 2,000 iterations and fails all standard fit criteria.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model fails to converge and all fit indices are far below acceptable thresholds. The general factor explains only 31\\% of variance, with subject-specific factors explaining 42\\% and residual variance at 27\\%. This indicates MMLU is neither unidimensional nor bifactor --- its latent structure may require 8--12 factors based on exploratory factor analysis, far fewer than 57 but far more than one. This result has implications beyond our work: any IRT-based analysis of MMLU (including anchor-item selection and efficient evaluation methods) operates under a misspecified model.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:rl_robustness}\n\nWe examine whether DIF patterns change when reasoning-RL models are included in the focal cohort (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_robustness}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & $N$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{DIF} & \\textbf{ConStat} & \\textbf{Min-K++} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 412 & 30.8 & 0.62 & 0.71 & 0.65 \\\\\nAlignment RL & 477 & 32.1 & 0.61 & 0.68 & 0.63 \\\\\n+Reasoning RL & 13 & 31.5 & 0.58 & 0.43 & 0.41 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{RL robustness. DIF/ConStat/Min-K++ columns show AUROC against Li labels. Reasoning-RL cohort ($N = 13$) is severely underpowered.}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\end{table}\n\nSFT-only and alignment-RL cohorts show comparable C\\% (30.8\\% vs.\\ 32.1\\%) with high item overlap (Jaccard $= 0.82$), suggesting DIF patterns are robust to alignment RL (N $= 477$, sufficient for group-level comparison). However, the reasoning-RL analysis is severely underpowered ($N = 13$). The TOST equivalence test fails ($p = 0.15$), and the DeLong test for DIF vs.\\ ConStat AUROC is non-significant ($p = 0.23$). We cannot draw conclusions about reasoning-RL robustness from this sample size.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:constat}\n\nWe compare MH-DIF against ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024), which detects contamination via semantic perturbation testing (Table~\\ref{tab:constat}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{AUROC} & \\textbf{FPR} & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{Unique} \\\\\n\\midrule\nConStat & 0.61 & 0.12 & 0.29 & 47 \\\\\nDIF (MH) & 0.52 & 0.31 & 0.31 & 31 \\\\\nOverlap & --- & --- & --- & $J = 0.18$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{ConStat vs.\\ DIF detection comparison against Li et al.\\ labels. AUROC is measured on 220 reformulated items.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat achieves higher AUROC (0.61 vs.\\ 0.52) and lower FPR (0.12 vs.\\ 0.31) against Li et al.\\ labels. The Jaccard overlap between flagged items is only 0.18, and the correlation between ConStat scores and $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}$ is 0.15. These methods detect different constructs: ConStat measures per-model perturbation sensitivity, while DIF measures group-level temporal differential functioning. The low overlap is expected given these different detection targets, but it also means that DIF's unique contribution cannot be assessed against a ground truth that ConStat better approximates.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:dvd}\n\nWe evaluate Detection via Distribution (DVD), which identifies contamination through bimodal response variance across temperature-sampled responses (Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Mode} & $n$ & \\textbf{DVD} & \\textbf{DIF} & \\textbf{Both} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBimodal variant & 34 & 28 & 6 & 4 \\\\\nHigh variance & 78 & 45 & 22 & 12 \\\\\nNormal & 388 & 31 & 125 & 18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{DVD detection results by contamination mode. DVD captures 82\\% (28/34) of bimodal-variant items; DIF captures only 18\\% (6/34).}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD achieves AUROC $= 0.58$ against Li labels with FPR $= 0.08$. The correlation between DVD and DIF signals is 0.09 --- near zero. DVD captures a contamination mode that DIF almost entirely misses: 82\\% of bimodal-variant items (28/34) are detected by DVD but only 18\\% by DIF. This exposes a structural blind spot: MH-DIF, which compares group-level response probabilities, cannot detect variant contamination where items have been paraphrased rather than verbatim memorized. As models increasingly train on paraphrased data, this limitation becomes more significant.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nWe combine DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals via calibrated weighted fusion and majority voting (Table~\\ref{tab:ensemble}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Config.} & \\textbf{AUROC} & \\textbf{FPR} & $w_{\\mathrm{DIF}}$ & $w_{\\mathrm{CS}}$ & $w_{\\mathrm{DVD}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.52 & 0.31 & 1.0 & 0 & 0 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.61 & 0.12 & 0 & 1.0 & 0 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.58 & 0.08 & 0 & 0 & 1.0 \\\\\nConStat+DVD & 0.65 & 0.10 & 0 & 0.57 & 0.43 \\\\\nFull (weighted) & 0.67 & 0.09 & 0.21 & 0.45 & 0.34 \\\\\nFull (majority) & 0.64 & 0.09 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ensemble ablation. Cross-validated weights assign DIF only 0.21 (vs.\\ ConStat 0.45, DVD 0.34). DIF's incremental AUROC over ConStat+DVD is $+0.02$.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe full weighted ensemble achieves AUROC $= 0.67$ with FPR $= 0.09$. However, DIF's contribution is marginal: cross-validated weight is 0.21, and removing DIF from the ensemble (ConStat+DVD) drops AUROC from 0.67 to 0.65 --- an incremental gain of only $+0.02$. ConStat carries the largest weight (0.45). This result positions DIF as the weakest signal in the ensemble, though we note that DIF provides group-level item diagnostics that per-model methods (ConStat, DVD) cannot, and that the Li et al.\\ ground truth may disadvantage group-level detectors by construction.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\n\\label{sec:item_gen}\n\nWe attempt to generate replacement items for DIF-flagged items using LLM generation with student simulation calibration (Table~\\ref{tab:item_gen}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Diff.\\ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Disc.\\ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Cov.} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt only & 0.18 & 0.12 & 0.45 \\\\\nStudent sim. & 0.52 & 0.31 & 0.68 \\\\\nTarget & $> 0.70$ & $> 0.50$ & $> 0.85$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Item generation calibration. Student simulation improves over prompt-only but all metrics fall below targets.}\n\\label{tab:item_gen}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation calibration improves difficulty correlation from $\\rho = 0.18$ to $0.52$ and construct coverage from 45\\% to 68\\%, but all metrics remain below targets ($\\rho > 0.70$, discrimination $> 0.50$, coverage $> 0.85$). The holdout ranking correlation is $\\rho = 0.91$ (target: 0.95). Moreover, 22\\% of generated replacement items themselves exhibit DIF, raising circularity concerns. Item generation at the quality level required for benchmark-maintaining psychometric properties remains an open challenge.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\nWe simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF evidence accumulation across successive administrations (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Admin.} & \\textbf{Power} ($\\delta{=}1.0$) & \\textbf{Power} ($\\delta{=}1.4$) & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & 0.68 & 0.04 \\\\\n2 & 0.52 & 0.79 & 0.05 \\\\\n3 & 0.64 & 0.87 & 0.06 \\\\\n4 & 0.71 & 0.92 & 0.08 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power simulation. Moderate power ($> 0.70$) for small effects ($\\delta = 1.0$) requires 4 administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\end{table}\n\nPower for detecting small DIF effects ($\\delta = 1.0$) improves from 0.38 (single administration) to 0.71 (four cumulative administrations). For operationally relevant effects ($\\delta = 1.4$), power reaches 0.87 after three administrations. However, this is a pure simulation with synthetic parameters; no empirical validation with real longitudinal data is available. The practical feasibility of four successive administrations ($> 2$ years) is questionable given the rapid pace of LLM development. FPR increases modestly from 0.04 to 0.08, remaining below the 0.10 threshold.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n\nWe measure benchmark validity metrics under progressive item replacement (Table~\\ref{tab:degradation}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Repl.\\ \\%} & \\textbf{Rank $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliab.} & \\textbf{Cov.} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0 & 1.000 & 0.97 & 1.00 \\\\\n5 & 0.998 & 0.96 & 0.97 \\\\\n10 & 0.994 & 0.94 & 0.93 \\\\\n20 & 0.983 & 0.91 & 0.86 \\\\\n31 & 0.972 & 0.88 & 0.79 \\\\\n50 & 0.941 & 0.82 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement. Rankings remain highly stable ($\\rho > 0.94$) even at 50\\% replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\end{table}\n\nRankings are remarkably stable: $\\rho = 0.983$ at 20\\% replacement and $\\rho = 0.941$ at 50\\%. Reliability remains above the 0.80 threshold ($0.82$) even at 50\\% replacement. The validity threshold (reliability $< 0.85$) is crossed at approximately 35\\% replacement. These results confirm the massive redundancy interpretation: MMLU contains 2--3$\\times$ more items than needed for stable rankings. This is a finding about MMLU's measurement efficiency, not a vindication of DIF's practical importance. Construct coverage drops to 0.71 at 50\\% replacement, but this reflects replacement quality rather than DIF detection utility.\n\n\\subsection{Odds Ratio Regression}\n\\label{sec:regression}\n\nWe investigate predictors of DIF status via logistic regression, revealing a Simpson's paradox (Table~\\ref{tab:regression}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Predictors} & $R^2_{\\mathrm{McF}}$ & \\textbf{Key OR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 0.0007 & 1.55 \\\\\nM2 & Domain $\\Delta$acc + items & 0.0423 & 0.73 (rev.) \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\\\\nM4 & Item $\\Delta$acc + items & 0.0434 & 0.92 (rev.) \\\\\n--- & --- & --- & Disc.\\ OR $= 1.54$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Logistic regression predicting DIF status. Simpson's paradox: domain accuracy change predicts DIF positively (M1: OR $= 1.55$) but negatively after controlling for item properties (M2: OR $= 0.73$).}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\end{table}\n\nA Simpson's paradox emerges: domain-level accuracy improvement positively predicts DIF (M1: OR $= 1.55$) but the direction \\emph{reverses} when item properties are included (M2: OR $= 0.73$). Item discrimination is the strongest predictor (OR $= 1.54$), and the full model explains only 4.3\\% of DIF variance ($R^2_{\\mathrm{McF}} = 0.043$). The remaining 95.7\\% is item-specific and unpredictable from aggregate statistics, supporting the characterization of DIF as an item-level diagnostic rather than a domain-level phenomenon. The Simpson's paradox reflects MMLU's complex hierarchical measurement structure and cautions against simple causal interpretations.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Measurement Invariance Violations as Primary Finding.}\nOur results converge on a central finding: MMLU systematically violates the measurement invariance assumptions implicit in its use as a benchmark. The 98\\% person-misfit rate under unidimensional IRT, combined with bifactor model failure (CFI $= 0.72$), indicates that neither unidimensional nor bifactor latent structures adequately describe MMLU's measurement properties. The 31.3\\% temporal DIF rate is a symptom of this deeper structural issue.\n\n\\paragraph{The Redundancy Paradox.}\nDIF flags 31\\% of items, yet removing them changes rankings minimally ($\\rho = 0.997$). This is not evidence that DIF is unimportant --- it reveals that MMLU contains massive measurement redundancy. Even at 50\\% item replacement, $\\rho$ remains above 0.94. The implication for benchmark design is that MMLU uses 2--3$\\times$ more items than needed for stable rankings.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark-Specific Measurement Properties.}\nCross-benchmark DIF variation (17.9--31.3\\%) is a feature, not a bug. Multi-domain benchmarks (MMLU: 31.3\\%) show higher DIF rates than single-domain ones (GSM8K: 18.9\\%, HellaSwag: 17.9\\%), likely reflecting the multidimensional construct that unidimensional IRT cannot adequately model. DIF characterizes \\emph{which} properties vary temporally for each benchmark.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF vs.\\ Per-Model Detectors.}\nDIF, ConStat, and DVD detect different constructs with low mutual overlap (Jaccard $= 0.09$--$0.18$). In an ensemble, DIF contributes the least weight (0.21 vs.\\ ConStat 0.45, DVD 0.34). This reflects the structural difference between group-level (DIF) and per-model (ConStat, DVD) detection, not necessarily DIF's inferiority. However, the low incremental AUROC ($+0.02$) is a genuine limitation.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K Anti-Correlation.}\nThe negative correlation between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination gap ($r = -0.478$) challenges a naive contamination interpretation. DIF identifies items with differential functioning after ability conditioning --- these may be items where high-ability models disproportionately improve, not contamination indicators. With $N = 33$, this result is exploratory.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\nThree primary limitations constrain our conclusions.\nFirst, 98\\% person-misfit under unidimensional IRT means the MH-DIF conditioning variable (total score) is derived from a misspecified model. While MH is robust to moderate model misspecification (Holland \\& Thayer, 1993), the severity of misfit here (CFI $= 0.72$, $|L_z| > 2$ for 98\\% of models) may inflate DIF detection rates. A multidimensional IRT or MIMIC model could address this, but our bifactor pilot failed to converge, leaving this as critical future work.\nSecond, DIF's blind spot for variant contamination (82\\% of bimodal items missed) limits its utility as contamination evolves toward paraphrase-based training data augmentation.\nThird, the semi-synthetic validation uses response flipping rather than realistic contamination injection, limiting ecological validity.\n\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe present the largest-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark stability, applying MH-DIF to 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items. Our primary finding is that MMLU systematically violates measurement invariance assumptions: 31.3\\% temporal DIF, 98\\% person-misfit under unidimensional IRT, and bifactor model failure (CFI $= 0.72$). These violations do not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho = 0.997$) but reveal massive measurement redundancy and benchmark-specific measurement properties (DIF range: 17.9--31.3\\% across six benchmarks). We provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) and demonstrate that DIF, ConStat, and DVD capture complementary signals. Future work should pursue multidimensional IRT models that better match MMLU's latent structure, enabling principled anchor-item selection and psychometrically grounded benchmark maintenance.\n\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":209,"oldLines":7,"newStart":209,"newLines":7,"lines":[" \\subsection{GSM8K Replication and GSM1K Correlation}"," \\label{sec:gsm8k}"," ","-We replicate temporal DIF on GSM8K and correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap \\cite{zhang2024gsm1k} as an external ground truth (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k}).","+We replicate temporal DIF on GSM8K and correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap (Zhang et al., 2024) as an external ground truth (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k})."," "," \\begin{table}[t]"," \\centering"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
